Creating RESTful APIs

This just means: organize your app's URLs so each CRUD action has its own clear route (address), following a pattern.

it's like labeling drawers in a kitchen so anyone (not just you) knows where the spoons are without asking. If every app follows the SAME labeling pattern, any developer can jump into any project and instantly understand it.

The standard pattern for a resource (let's say "dogs" 🐶):

Action	Method	URL	What it does
Index	GET	/dogs	Show ALL dogs
New	GET	/dogs/new	Show a FORM to add a dog
Create	POST	/dogs	Actually SAVE the new dog
Show	GET	/dogs/:id	Show ONE specific dog
Edit	GET	/dogs/:id/edit	Show a FORM to edit a dog
Update	PATCH/PUT	/dogs/:id	Actually SAVE the edited dog
Destroy	DELETE	/dogs/:id	Delete a dog
#Index Route

What it does: Shows the list of ALL items.

GET /dogs
it's like opening the fridge and seeing everything inside at once — not picking one item yet, just the full list.

js
app.get('/dogs', (req, res) => {
  res.render('index.ejs', { dogs: allDogs });
});

#Create & New Route

These two ALWAYS come as a pair — one shows a form, the other saves what you typed.

New Route (the empty form)
GET /dogs/new
this is the blank piece of paper you get before filling anything in. It shows an HTML form.

js
app.get('/dogs/new', (req, res) => {
  res.render('new.ejs');
});

#Create Route (actually saving it)
POST /dogs
his happens after you hit "submit" on that form. The data you typed gets sent here and gets SAVED into the database/array.

js
app.post('/dogs', (req, res) => {
  const newDog = req.body; // data from the form
  dogs.push(newDog); // save it
  res.redirect('/dogs'); // go back to see the list
});

⚠️ New = shows the form. Create = saves the form. Super common mix-up — don't confuse them!


#Redirect

What it does: Sends the browser to a DIFFERENT url automatically, instead of showing a page directly.
imagine you knock on the wrong door, and the person says "oh they moved, go to the house next door" — and you just walk there automatically without thinking. That's a redirect.

js
res.redirect('/dogs'); // "hey browser, go visit /dogs instead"
After you CREATE or UPDATE or DELETE something, you don't want to just sit on that same "processing" page — you redirect back to the Index page so the user sees the updated list.
#. Show Route

What it does: Shows details of ONE specific item, using its unique ID.

GET /dogs/:id
 : if Index is "open the fridge and see everything," Show is "pick up ONE yogurt cup and read its label closely."

js
app.get('/dogs/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = req.params.id;
  const dog = dogs.find(d => d.id === id);
  res.render('show.ejs', { dog });
});
:id is a placeholder — it means "whatever value shows up here, grab it and call it id.

Creating IDs (UUID)

Problem: Every item needs a unique ID so we can find it later (for Show, Update, Delete). You can't just use random numbers because two items might accidentally get the same number.

Solution: UUID = Universally Unique Identifier. It's a library that generates a super long, random, practically-never-repeats-itself ID like:

110ec58a-a0f2-4ac4-8393-c866d813b8d1
it's like giving every single kid on Earth their own super-long secret nickname so NO TWO KIDS ever accidentally have the same one — even if there are billions of kids.

js
const { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid');

const newDog = {
  id: uuidv4(),  // generates a unique ID
  name: req.body.name
};

#Update Route

What it does: Actually saves the edited version of an item.

PATCH /dogs/:id
you erased something on your notebook page and wrote the new version — Update is the act of saving that fixed page.

js
app.patch('/dogs/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = req.params.id;
  const dog = dogs.find(d => d.id === id);
  dog.name = req.body.name; // overwrite with new data
  res.redirect(`/dogs/${id}`); // go see the updated item
});

 Browsers don't naturally support PATCH/DELETE from HTML forms — that's why courses often use a package called method-override to "fake" it.

#Edit Route

What it does: Shows a form pre-filled with the existing data, so you can change it.

GET /dogs/:id/edit
app.get('/dogs/:id/edit', (req, res) => {
  const id = req.params.id;
  const dog = dogs.find(d => d.id === id);
  res.render('edit.ejs', { dog });
});
#Destroy Route

What it does: Deletes an item completely.

DELETE /dogs/:id
app.delete('/dogs/:id', (req, res) => {
  const id = req.params.id;
  dogs = dogs.filter(d => d.id !== id); // keep everyone EXCEPT this one
  res.redirect('/dogs'); // go back to the list
})

